        "IQS_05": {
            "question_id": "IQS_05",
            "status": "scored",
            "score": 7,
            "description": "Snowflake's core competitive advantages are supported by identifiable evidence but face meaningful replication pressure. (1) Architecture: The multi-cluster shared-data architecture with independent scaling of storage and compute is repeatedly cited as the primary technical moat, validated by ~21.76% data-warehouse market share (vs Redshift 16.84%, BigQuery 13.79%) and 35.8% YoY revenue growth in FY24. (2) Multi-cloud neutrality: Running natively on AWS, Azure, and GCP is a structural advantage over hyperscaler-tied competitors (Redshift, BigQuery, Synapse), evidenced by enterprise adoption (e.g., Ally Financial). (3) Secure Data Sharing + Snowgrid network: Connects 9,800+ organizations across clouds without data movement; Marketplace hosts 2,000+ live data products with ~115,000 monthly visitors, creating genuine network effects. (4) Managed-service simplicity: 'Zero-ops' positioning is concrete and difficult to fully replicate cost-effectively by competitors focused on different segments. Limitations reducing the score: (a) Databricks has largely matched the separation-of-concerns architecture and arguably leads in ML/AI workloads; (b) hyperscalers continue expanding native offerings (e.g., BigQuery serverless, Redshift RA3/MDL) and have unmatched R&D budgets; (c) Iceberg Tables and Polaris/Open Catalog reduce lock-in by enabling portability; (d) Snowflake itself notes the proprietary engine is 'less open and controllable than a lakehouse'; (e) customer reports cite cost unpredictability and weaker unstructured-data/streaming support. Overall, evidence supports a real but narrowing moat — durable in the near term but not insurmountable at reasonable cost by well-resourced competitors.",
            "source_urls": [
                "https://www.incentius.com/blog-posts/snowflake-a-game-changer-in-cloud-data-warehousing/",
