        "IQS_06": {
            "question_id": "IQS_06",
            "status": "scored",
            "score": 8,
            "description": "Snowflake's competitive position is highly stable and reinforced. Evidence: (1) Market-leading independent cloud data warehouse vendor with ~18.3-20.2% share in 2025, roughly 2x Databricks (~8.7-15.2%) and the largest non-hyperscaler player. (2) Strong customer base: 11,000+ customers, 745 Forbes Global 2000, 580 customers spending >$1M annually (FY25); Q3 FY26 saw 688 $1M+ customers; net revenue retention 125-126% indicates deep expansion. (3) FY25 product revenue $3.46B (+30% YoY), FY26 product revenue guidance $4.28B (+24% YoY); Q2 FY26 revenue $1.145B (+31.8% YoY) - growth has remained above 26% for four consecutive years. (4) Defensible moat: high switching costs (integrated workflows, trained teams), 47 regional multi-cloud deployments, unique zero-copy data sharing, Snowflake Marketplace ecosystem. (5) AI/Data Cloud expansion (Cortex AI, Snowflake Intelligence, 6,100+ accounts using AI weekly) and partnerships with Anthropic, AWS, Microsoft, ServiceNow strengthen stickiness. (6) Trend is stable-to-strengthening: 5-year revenue CAGR 57.4%, share has held/grown despite intensifying competition. Concerns (not enough to reduce score below 8): Databricks growing faster (~55-65% YoY vs Snowflake's 28-30%), hyperscaler bundling pressure (Redshift ~22-25%, BigQuery ~15-18%, Synapse ~10-17%), growth rate gradually decelerating, and ongoing GAAP losses limiting defensive flexibility. Overall trend supports continued stability of competitive position.",
